<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        THE FILTER CHIP AND THE ROW IT LIVES IN - `A5`. Styles and templates only, not one value.

        THE ELEVENTH THEME FILE, AND THE ARGUMENT IS A PALETTE STUDY RATHER THAN A LINE COUNT. On
        2026-09-01 the owner asked whether buttons ought to be a different colour from filters,
        because everything in the chrome blended. The study measured why: a chip was 47 pixels tall
        and the action button under it was 48, same corner, same text size - so seventeen controls
        that change a QUESTION and seven that open a PLAN were the same silhouette to within a
        pixel. The answer he approved is that the families are told apart by SHAPE, and that this
        product's one saturated colour is spent once.

        SO A CHIP IS NOW ITS OWN FAMILY, AND A FAMILY DESERVES A FILE. Controls.xaml keeps the
        ordinary controls - the field somebody types into, the box somebody ticks, the items of the
        column picker, and the button that matters most on a screen. This keeps the one control in
        this window that is neither: a pill with no fill, which is a token rather than a control you
        press. The immediate cause is the same as every earlier seam - the primary action's template
        could not be added while Controls.xaml stood at 369 against a ceiling of 376.

        WHAT CHANGED IN THE MOVE, so it is not hidden in a file that reads as a copy: the chip is
        TRANSPARENT AT REST and its outline is SurfaceChipEdge rather than SurfacePanelEdge. A chip
        that is off now has nothing but that outline to say it is a control, so the outline answers
        to SC 1.4.11 in full and measures 3.20 where the old one measured 1.84.

        MERGED AFTER THE VALUE FILES AND AFTER Text.xaml, which the group template reaches for
        SubduedText through StaticResource - resolved WHILE THIS FILE IS BEING READ.
    -->

    <!--
        A CHIP THAT IS ON HAS TO LOOK ON FROM ACROSS THE ROOM - backlog 163, and it was recorded as
        a decision rather than an oversight: on 2026-08-11 Controls.xaml stood exactly on its own
        ceiling, so a chip style would have forced a second split of the theme inside one session.
        The theme has been split since, so the reason is spent.

        WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE DEFAULT. WPF UI's ToggleButton marks "checked" with a fill a shade
        lighter than "not checked", against a window that is already dark. Whether a filter is on is
        the single thing this row exists to say, and saying it in a shade is the same failure as
        `docs/11` complaint 1 - the most important state on screen carried by the least visible
        difference.

        SurfaceSelected AND TextOnSurface RATHER THAN A NEW PAIR. Those two are the row selection
        colours - already declared, already carrying a contrast threshold `ContrastGuards` enforces,
        and already meaning "this one is picked" everywhere else in this window. A new brush would
        be a new threshold to argue for a meaning the theme already has a colour for.

        BasedOn THEIRS, WHICH IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10` OBEYED RATHER THAN AVOIDED: their style sets
        OverridesDefaultStyle and supplies a whole template, so a style of our own that did not
        inherit it would silently throw away every other state - hover, pressed, disabled, focus.
        What we override is three brushes it template-binds.

        AND IT IS CHECKED ON THE PIXEL, not on the property. The same library hardcodes a
        background inside its DataGrid cell template, where a Background setter is dead
        unconditionally - so "the setter is there" is not evidence about the screen.
    -->
    <!--
        AND THE CHIP STOPPED BORROWING THE LIBRARY'S BUTTON ON 2026-09-01, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT
        REPAIR FROM THE ONE ABOVE AND HAS ITS OWN MEASUREMENT.

        WHAT THE MEASUREMENT SAID. Read off the automation tree with the window at 1650x1050: a
        filter chip was 47 pixels tall, and the action button under it - the one that opens a plan to
        stop a service - was 48. Same rounded rectangle, same corner, same text size, same height to
        within a pixel. Seventeen of the first and seven of the second, stacked in five rows, which
        is what makes the top of that window read as a wall rather than as two kinds of thing.

        THIS IS `docs/11` COMPLAINT 14 ONE CONTROL OVER, AND ITS RULE IS THE ONE FOLLOWED HERE. That
        one was the scope switch borrowing the chip's appearance, and the owner's words were "they
        read as a list choice, they should not look the same as a chip because it misleads". The rule
        written down from it: when the rejection is about what a look MEANS, the repair is a whole
        visual family, not a different shade of the family that is already wrong.

        SO THE TEMPLATE IS WRITTEN OUT, WHICH IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10` MET THE WAY ScopeBar.xaml AND
        OverviewNumber MEET IT. A corner radius is the strongest of the three signals and there is no
        setter for it: WPF UI's ToggleButton template resolves its own from a library resource, so
        the only ways to reach it are to overwrite that resource for the whole application - every
        control in the window - or to own the template. The cost is named and it is real: hover,
        pressed, checked and disabled are this file's job now, and all four are below.

        THE PART THAT IS KEPT FROM THE STYLE THIS REPLACES, because it was right: a chip that is ON
        wears SurfaceSelected and TextOnSurface rather than a new pair. Those are the row selection
        colours, they already carry thresholds ContrastGuards enforces, and they already mean "this
        one is picked" everywhere else in this window. WPF UI's own "checked" is a shade lighter than
        "not checked" against a dark window, which is `docs/11` complaint 1 exactly: the one state
        this row exists to show, shown in the least visible difference there is.

        A CHECKED CHIP UNDER THE POINTER BRIGHTENS ITS OUTLINE RATHER THAN ITS FILL, and that is a
        choice made to avoid inventing a colour. A lighter accent would be a new brush with a new
        threshold to argue, for a state that lasts as long as somebody's hand is still. White is
        already the pairing on that fill, so the outline borrows what is there.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ChipFocusRing">
        <Setter Property="Control.Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate>
                    <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerChip}" />
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="FilterChip" TargetType="ToggleButton">
        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource ChipFocusRing}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadChip}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                    <Border x:Name="Pill"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceChipEdge}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderTile}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerChip}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <!--
                            A ContentPresenter, SO THE CHIP'S TEXT IS THE THEME'S DEFAULT SIZE RATHER
                            THAN THE 13 THIS STYLE WOULD HAVE ASKED FOR - a defeat, recorded rather
                            than hidden. Four attempts, all measured: `docs/10` trap 16 has them, and
                            backlog 272 carries what it costs. The short version is that an implicit
                            TextBlock style outranks every inherited size, and the one thing that
                            does win takes sixteen elements out of the automation tree.
                        -->
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <!--
                        ORDER IS THE BEHAVIOUR HERE, not tidiness: a later trigger wins, so "on"
                        has to come after "pointer is here" or a checked chip would go grey under
                        the hand, which is the one state this row exists to show, unshown.
                    -->
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Pill" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Pill" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Pill" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <MultiTrigger>
                            <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                <Condition Property="IsChecked" Value="True" />
                                <Condition Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True" />
                            </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                            <Setter TargetName="Pill" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </MultiTrigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Pill" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE FACET: ITS NAME, THEN ITS CHIPS. Here rather than inside MainWindow.xaml because what a
        chip looks like is decided four lines above, and a template drawing chips is the same
        subject - the window was carrying a nested ItemsControl whose only job was to apply the
        style declared here. It also kept the window under the markup ceiling without shortening a
        single reason, which is what a seam is for.

        The hint sits on every group label rather than once beside the row, because the sentence is
        about the boundary BETWEEN groups and a person reads whichever label they arrive at. It is
        a tooltip rather than a second line: it explains a mechanism somebody discovers by clicking
        once, which is worth having and not worth a line of a window whose job is 810 rows.

        THE GROUP LABEL IS WHAT COMPLAINT 7 ASKED FOR, and it waited for this row on purpose.
        docs/11 closed that complaint in half on 2026-08-05 and said the other half - a label over
        the controls - was deliberately not built, because A5 was going to arrive and rewrite the
        row. It has, so the label arrives with the row it labels rather than being invented twice.

        THREE GROUPS SINCE 2026-08-12. Why these three and what each one may contain is decided in
        FilterChips.cs, with the measurement that settled the boundary - it is not repeated here.
        The single "Filters" label is gone: it named the row rather than any question in it.

        THE LABELS LIVE INSIDE THE CHIPS CONTAINER ON PURPOSE. tools/window-journey tells the labels
        of controls apart from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by
        asking whether the chips' ItemsControl is an ancestor. A label outside it would arrive as
        one of the window's sentences.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="FilterGroupTemplate">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="{StaticResource MarginGroup}">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label}"
                       ToolTip="{Binding Hint}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Chips}">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <WrapPanel />
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>

                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate>
                        <ToggleButton Margin="{StaticResource MarginChip}"
                                      Style="{StaticResource FilterChip}"
                                      Content="{Binding Label}"
                                      ToolTip="{Binding Member}"
                                      IsChecked="{Binding IsOn, Mode=TwoWay}" />
                    </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
        </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        THE MARK ON THE CONTROL THAT FOLDS THIS ROW AWAY, 2026-09-02, and it is here rather than in
        Controls.xaml because what it belongs to is this row.

        WHAT IT REPLACES AND WHY THE REPLACEMENT IS A SHAPE. The Filters button said whether the row
        was open by wearing a DIFFERENT FILL when it was - and the fill it wore measured 1.31 against
        the window with an edge at 1.84, which `docs/11` already records as the ratio at which you
        cannot tell "the colour is not arriving" from "the colour arrives and nobody sees it". The
        owner's words were that it looks like a random element. It was the least visible control in
        the window, carrying the state of the largest block in it.

        So the state moves off the fill and onto a triangle, and the button goes back to looking
        exactly like Columns and Show every instance - the two beside it that also change what the
        LIST shows rather than what the query asks.

        A Path RATHER THAN A GLYPH, AND BOTH REASONS ARE MEASURED ONES FROM THIS PROJECT. A
        character would need a font that carries it on Windows Server, which is a dependency nobody
        would notice failing until the window was on one - and a TextBlock in CONTENT gets an
        automation node of its own, which is trap 16 seen from the other side: sixteen texts
        appeared in the tree the last time this product put one there. A Shape gets no peer, so the
        button keeps one name and the tree keeps one node.

        THE FILL FOLLOWS THE BUTTON'S OWN FOREGROUND rather than naming a brush, so the mark can
        never be the one part of the control that does not answer to hover or to being disabled.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="FilterDisclosure" TargetType="Path">
        <Setter Property="Fill"
                Value="{Binding Foreground, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ToggleButton}}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />
        <Setter Property="Data" Value="M 0,0 L 8,0 L 4,5 Z" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsChecked, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ToggleButton}}"
                         Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Data" Value="M 0,0 L 5,4 L 0,8 Z" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
